No — it's the immediate parent commit (`HEAD~1`), not the merge-base. Look at lines 828-834 of `manual_commit_hooks.go`:

```go
var parentTree *object.Tree
if commit.NumParents() > 0 {
    if parent, err := commit.Parent(0); err == nil {
        if t, err := parent.Tree(); err == nil {
            parentTree = t
        }
    }
}
```

`commit.Parent(0)` is just the first parent of the HEAD commit — the commit right before this one. So `filesChangedInCommit(commit, headTree, parentTree)` computes "what files changed in *this specific commit*" (HEAD vs HEAD~1), not "what changed across the whole branch."

The **base tree** in attribution is different — that's `state.BaseCommit` (or `state.AttributionBaseCommit`), which is the commit HEAD was at when the session started. That one is more like a merge-base concept, but it's session-scoped, not branch-scoped.

